WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.964 --> 00:02.900 align:center line:81%
Christ is the Good Shepherd.

00:03.433 --> 00:06.103 align:center line:81%
Each one of the flock is precious to Him.

00:06.904 --> 00:09.673 align:center line:79%
He patterned shepherding
and taught us by word

00:09.673 --> 00:11.942 align:center line:81%
and deed the qualities of a good shepherd,

00:11.942 --> 00:18.248 align:center line:79%
including knowing your sheep by name,
loving them, finding those that are lost,

00:18.248 --> 00:21.451 align:center line:79%
feeding, and ultimately
leading them back home again.

00:22.252 --> 00:26.123 align:center line:79%
He expects us to do the same
as His under-shepherds.

00:27.024 --> 00:30.994 align:center line:79%
We can learn much about ministering
the Lord’s way from the ancient prophet—

00:30.994 --> 00:33.297 align:center line:81%
and exceptional shepherd—Moroni.

00:34.097 --> 00:39.202 align:center line:79%
He lived during very difficult times,
not having the advantages of cell phones,

00:39.202 --> 00:43.941 align:center line:79%
computers, or the internet.
But he managed to keep track of the sheep.

00:44.474 --> 00:50.013 align:center line:79%
How was this done? We get a glimpse
into his methodology in Moroni 6.

00:51.048 --> 00:55.319 align:center line:79%
There we read that members “were
numbered among the people of the church

00:55.319 --> 00:58.589 align:center line:81%
of Christ; and their names were taken,

00:58.889 --> 01:01.091 align:center line:81%
that they might be remembered

01:01.425 --> 01:05.362 align:center line:79%
and nourished by the good word
of God, to keep them in the right way.”

01:05.929 --> 01:08.999 align:center line:79%
The Church did “meet together
oft, to fast and to pray,

01:09.333 --> 01:15.238 align:center line:79%
and to speak one with another
concerning the welfare of their souls.”

01:16.039 --> 01:21.078 align:center line:81%
For Moroni, it was all about people—names!

01:21.945 --> 01:25.482 align:center line:79%
He practiced the principle
of “counting and accounting”

01:25.782 --> 01:27.584 align:center line:81%
so that all would be remembered.

01:28.285 --> 01:31.188 align:center line:79%
Any who struggled
or wandered were noticed,

01:31.521 --> 01:36.560 align:center line:79%
allowing the Saints to discuss their
welfare in councils. Like the shepherd

01:36.560 --> 01:39.229 align:center line:81%
who left the 90 and 9 (safe and secure,

01:39.262 --> 01:42.799 align:center line:79%
I am certain), and went
after the one that was lost,

01:43.166 --> 01:46.269 align:center line:79%
we have been asked
to be just as aware of our flocks,

01:46.536 --> 01:52.909 align:center line:79%
to notice and remember and go and do
likewise. As a mission leader in India,

01:53.243 --> 01:56.747 align:center line:79%
I recall asking a young branch
president about some of his goals

01:56.747 --> 02:00.283 align:center line:79%
for the coming year.
“How many men will you prepare

02:00.283 --> 02:02.452 align:center line:81%
to receive the Melchizedek Priesthood?”

02:02.986 --> 02:05.889 align:center line:81%
His immediate response was, “Seven.”

02:06.590 --> 02:07.891 align:center line:81%
Now I wondered from where

02:07.891 --> 02:11.194 align:center line:79%
in the ether he had conjured
up that very specific number.

02:11.928 --> 02:13.563 align:center line:81%
Before I could respond,

02:13.697 --> 02:15.499 align:center line:81%
he produced a piece of paper

02:15.832 --> 02:19.269 align:center line:79%
with numbers from one through
seven written down the side.

02:19.936 --> 02:23.006 align:center line:81%
The first five lines had names on them,

02:23.407 --> 02:27.144 align:center line:79%
real people that he and his elders
quorum were going to invite

02:27.144 --> 02:30.947 align:center line:79%
and encourage to have the blessings
of the priesthood in their lives.

02:31.748 --> 02:36.253 align:center line:79%
Of course, I had to ask about
the empty lines six and seven.

02:37.254 --> 02:40.891 align:center line:79%
“Oh, president,” he said,
shaking his head sympathetically.

02:41.358 --> 02:46.663 align:center line:79%
“Surely we will baptize
at least two men in the first of the year

02:46.997 --> 02:50.333 align:center line:79%
who could have the priesthood
by the end of the year.”

02:51.134 --> 02:57.374 align:center line:79%
This superb leader understood
the principle of counting and accounting.

02:58.842 --> 03:03.680 align:center line:79%
Christ has organized His church in such
a way that it should be difficult

03:03.780 --> 03:08.185 align:center line:81%
to forget a soul, for each is dear to Him.

03:09.386 --> 03:13.457 align:center line:79%
Every individual in a ward,
regardless of age or gender,

03:13.623 --> 03:16.860 align:center line:81%
has a multitude of stewards—shepherds—

03:16.993 --> 03:20.897 align:center line:79%
who are tasked with looking
after them, with remembering.

03:22.032 --> 03:26.670 align:center line:79%
A young man, for example, has
assigned to his well-being a bishopric,

03:26.837 --> 03:31.074 align:center line:79%
ministering brothers,
adult youth advisors, seminary teachers,

03:31.074 --> 03:33.744 align:center line:81%
quorum presidencies, and then some,

03:34.111 --> 03:39.182 align:center line:79%
all serving as safety nets
strung up firmly beneath that youth

03:39.182 --> 03:40.917 align:center line:81%
to catch him if he falls.

03:41.852 --> 03:46.823 align:center line:79%
Even if just one net is properly
positioned, that young man will be safe,

03:46.957 --> 03:48.792 align:center line:81%
noticed, and remembered.

03:49.493 --> 03:53.897 align:center line:79%
And yet, often we find
nary a net in place.

03:54.731 --> 03:59.503 align:center line:79%
People wander off routinely
into the mists and no one notices.

04:00.504 --> 04:02.239 align:center line:81%
How can we be better shepherds?

04:03.039 --> 04:05.642 align:center line:81%
We can learn to count and account.

04:06.443 --> 04:10.313 align:center line:79%
The Church supplies us
with reports and tools to do just that—

04:10.313 --> 04:14.484 align:center line:79%
to remember. The quarterly
report is a prime example.

04:14.818 --> 04:19.823 align:center line:79%
It allows us to count and account
for each member multiple times,

04:20.023 --> 04:24.060 align:center line:79%
and to notice those that go
missing or need our help and our love.

04:24.861 --> 04:27.330 align:center line:79%
The action and interview
list identifies those

04:27.330 --> 04:31.935 align:center line:79%
who require our attention right now,
as does the Temple Recommend

04:31.968 --> 04:33.603 align:center line:81%
Status Report and others.

04:34.037 --> 04:40.677 align:center line:79%
These counting and accounting tools focus
us in on people. Who needs a calling,

04:41.244 --> 04:45.282 align:center line:79%
a priesthood advancement, or help
taking a family name to the temple?

04:45.782 --> 04:48.952 align:center line:79%
Who could we be helping
to prepare for a full-time mission?

04:50.253 --> 04:51.988 align:center line:81%
Who went missing this month?

04:52.756 --> 04:55.392 align:center line:81%
These tools help us to remember people.

04:56.626 --> 05:00.964 align:center line:79%
I knew a family that took an assignment
in Africa. On their very first Sunday,

05:00.964 --> 05:02.732 align:center line:81%
they walked into the only Church unit

05:02.732 --> 05:06.069 align:center line:79%
in the country, where they were
greeted enthusiastically.

05:06.536 --> 05:07.938 align:center line:81%
By the end of the morning,

05:07.938 --> 05:11.474 align:center line:79%
the man's wife had been called
as the Relief Society president

05:11.474 --> 05:13.543 align:center line:81%
and he as the Young Men’s leader.

05:14.778 --> 05:19.716 align:center line:79%
He asked an exhausted looking branch
president how many young men there were.

05:20.550 --> 05:24.421 align:center line:79%
This faithful, first-generation leader
pointed to the back of the sacrament hall

05:24.421 --> 05:26.590 align:center line:81%
and said, “Those two right there.”

05:27.724 --> 05:32.696 align:center line:79%
The man was appropriately skeptical,
so he took a branch roster home, quickly

05:32.696 --> 05:36.633 align:center line:79%
noting that there were
actually 20 young men on the list.

05:37.234 --> 05:41.004 align:center line:79%
He returned to the branch
president and asked for two dynamic,

05:41.004 --> 05:44.274 align:center line:79%
bilingual young adults
to serve as his counselors,

05:44.274 --> 05:48.879 align:center line:79%
and then sat down with them
and the two boys to review the names.

05:49.913 --> 05:53.683 align:center line:79%
Then these diligent
young people went to work.

05:54.818 --> 06:00.423 align:center line:79%
Over the next few months,
they found every boy listed. Name by name,

06:00.557 --> 06:05.061 align:center line:79%
those lost sheep were welcomed back
by their peers and fed spiritually

06:05.295 --> 06:07.964 align:center line:81%
and physically. Within a year,

06:08.098 --> 06:13.036 align:center line:79%
on any given Sunday, there was an average
of 21 young men in attendance.

06:13.436 --> 06:16.873 align:center line:79%
Thank goodness for young
men who counted and accounted.

06:17.941 --> 06:20.443 align:center line:79%
A dear friend of mine,
as a young graduate student,

06:20.443 --> 06:24.981 align:center line:79%
moved with his family to a large
American city to continue his education.

06:25.015 --> 06:28.418 align:center line:79%
He was immediately called to preside
over the elders quorum.

06:29.152 --> 06:32.622 align:center line:79%
A little nervous about his first interview
with the stake president,

06:32.622 --> 06:34.958 align:center line:81%
he was determined to go prepared.

06:34.958 --> 06:38.662 align:center line:79%
He told the stake
president that he had three goals

06:38.662 --> 06:39.863 align:center line:81%
for the upcoming year.

06:40.196 --> 06:42.532 align:center line:81%
First, 90 percent ministering;

06:42.832 --> 06:47.270 align:center line:79%
second, a substantive gospel
lesson each week; and third,

06:47.637 --> 06:50.540 align:center line:79%
a well-planned quorum
activity every month.

06:51.508 --> 06:55.578 align:center line:79%
Smiling at my friend,
this wise stake president asked,

06:56.313 --> 06:58.815 align:center line:81%
“Can you name a less-active quorum member

06:59.115 --> 07:03.053 align:center line:79%
who you could help get
to the temple with his family this year?”

07:03.053 --> 07:05.755 align:center line:81%
Well, that caught my friend by surprise.

07:05.755 --> 07:08.358 align:center line:79%
He thought carefully
and came up with a name.

07:08.358 --> 07:11.194 align:center line:79%
“Write that down,”
directed the stake president.

07:11.995 --> 07:17.200 align:center line:79%
Then this experienced leader asked
the same question three more times

07:17.400 --> 07:19.269 align:center line:81%
and the interview was over.

07:20.270 --> 07:22.539 align:center line:79%
This young man walked out
of that interview

07:22.539 --> 07:27.110 align:center line:79%
having learned one of his greatest lessons
on leadership and ministering.

07:27.644 --> 07:31.481 align:center line:79%
He went into the interview with programs,
lessons, and activities.

07:31.982 --> 07:35.085 align:center line:81%
He walked out with names,

07:36.052 --> 07:40.890 align:center line:79%
and those four names subsequently
became a major focus of his ministry

07:40.890 --> 07:42.192 align:center line:81%
and that of his quorum.

07:43.526 --> 07:47.397 align:center line:79%
As a mission leader, I visited one
of my branches one Sunday morning.

07:47.797 --> 07:51.634 align:center line:79%
I noticed that the branch president
kept taking a card out of his pocket

07:51.634 --> 07:56.373 align:center line:79%
and writing on it. I decided to ask
him about that after the closing prayer.

07:57.173 --> 08:00.844 align:center line:79%
Once the meeting ended
and before I could inquire about the card,

08:00.844 --> 08:05.048 align:center line:79%
the branch mission leader raced to the
podium where he was handed the paper.

08:05.682 --> 08:08.184 align:center line:79%
I quickly followed
this enthusiastic leader

08:08.184 --> 08:13.556 align:center line:79%
to his weekly branch missionary
coordination meeting. Before they started,

08:13.690 --> 08:15.592 align:center line:81%
he took the paper out of his pocket.

08:16.192 --> 08:19.562 align:center line:81%
It was filled with the names of members

08:19.562 --> 08:22.632 align:center line:79%
who had been missing
from sacrament meeting.

08:22.632 --> 08:27.003 align:center line:79%
Within a few minutes, each member
of the council had selected a name or two,

08:27.003 --> 08:31.841 align:center line:79%
committing to visit them that very
day to make sure that they were okay

08:32.242 --> 08:35.145 align:center line:79%
and to let them know
that they had been missed.

08:35.145 --> 08:37.580 align:center line:81%
Now that is counting and accounting.

08:38.882 --> 08:43.219 align:center line:79%
I recall a district hours by
jet from the nearest temple,

08:43.219 --> 08:46.456 align:center line:79%
where maintaining a current
recommend was a high priority,

08:46.656 --> 08:50.360 align:center line:79%
despite the fact that it
would likely never be used.

08:50.994 --> 08:52.562 align:center line:81%
The first Sunday of each month,

08:52.562 --> 08:57.267 align:center line:79%
leaders used their counting tools
to account for their endowed members.

08:57.667 --> 09:00.403 align:center line:79%
If they found that a recommend
was soon to expire,

09:00.603 --> 09:04.274 align:center line:79%
the executive secretary
would schedule a renewal interview.

09:04.974 --> 09:07.944 align:center line:79%
People with expired
recommends were counseled over,

09:08.178 --> 09:11.915 align:center line:79%
then sought out to assist them
in returning to the covenant path.

09:12.782 --> 09:16.586 align:center line:79%
I asked how many of their endowed
members had a current recommend.

09:16.586 --> 09:24.294 align:center line:79%
The answer was an astounding
98.6 percent. When asked about the six

09:24.494 --> 09:26.296 align:center line:81%
whose recommends had expired,

09:26.830 --> 09:30.233 align:center line:79%
the leaders were able
to identify them by name

09:30.567 --> 09:34.504 align:center line:79%
and describe to me the efforts
being made to get them back.

09:35.872 --> 09:38.808 align:center line:79%
A few years ago, my family
moved back to the States.

09:39.008 --> 09:41.611 align:center line:81%
We were excited to attend the Church here

09:41.845 --> 09:46.850 align:center line:79%
after 26 amazing years
in smaller, more isolated units.

09:47.417 --> 09:49.285 align:center line:81%
I was called as a ward missionary.

09:49.819 --> 09:53.490 align:center line:79%
We had a great ward mission
leader and were doing exciting things

09:53.490 --> 09:55.024 align:center line:81%
and teaching wonderful people.

09:56.059 --> 09:59.295 align:center line:79%
I asked to attend a ward
council meeting to observe

09:59.496 --> 10:03.166 align:center line:79%
and to get their help
with the friends we were working with.

10:03.166 --> 10:07.804 align:center line:79%
I was surprised when all that we discussed
was an upcoming ward activity.

10:08.805 --> 10:11.074 align:center line:79%
I approached the ward
mission leader afterwards

10:11.074 --> 10:15.612 align:center line:79%
and opined that he didn't get the chance
to return and report on our people.

10:16.246 --> 10:19.616 align:center line:81%
His response? “Oh, I never get to report.”

10:20.850 --> 10:26.689 align:center line:79%
I contrasted that to a branch
council meeting in Lahore,

10:26.923 --> 10:30.793 align:center line:79%
Pakistan, that I had attended
just weeks before.

10:31.661 --> 10:34.864 align:center line:79%
This little group sat
around a small table together

10:35.331 --> 10:40.103 align:center line:79%
and all they talked
about were people—names.

10:40.737 --> 10:44.507 align:center line:79%
Each leader reported
on their stewardship and the individuals

10:44.507 --> 10:47.210 align:center line:79%
and families that they were
concerned about.

10:47.210 --> 10:49.646 align:center line:81%
All had the chance to add their thoughts

10:49.646 --> 10:53.483 align:center line:79%
on the best ways that they could bless
those being discussed.

10:53.883 --> 10:56.553 align:center line:81%
Plans were made and assignments given.

10:57.220 --> 10:59.255 align:center line:81%
What a brilliant lesson in counting

10:59.255 --> 11:03.893 align:center line:79%
and accounting by name from
our first-generation brothers and

11:03.893 --> 11:07.497 align:center line:81%
sisters. In the Church of Jesus Christ,

11:08.097 --> 11:12.502 align:center line:79%
we have been instructed by prophets
past and prophets present

11:12.936 --> 11:15.271 align:center line:81%
and by the pattern set by our Savior

11:15.305 --> 11:18.708 align:center line:81%
how to minister. We take names,

11:19.008 --> 11:23.546 align:center line:79%
we remember, and we counsel
over the welfare of souls.

11:24.180 --> 11:27.984 align:center line:79%
Leaders who do this will never
run out of agenda items

11:27.984 --> 11:29.385 align:center line:81%
in their council meetings.

11:30.186 --> 11:32.789 align:center line:79%
The principle of counting
and accounting works.

11:33.156 --> 11:34.490 align:center line:81%
It is the Lord's way.

11:35.225 --> 11:38.194 align:center line:81%
We can do better. To God,

11:38.361 --> 11:42.932 align:center line:79%
who created the universe
and rules over all, this work,

11:43.333 --> 11:46.636 align:center line:81%
His work and glory, is very personal.

11:47.103 --> 11:51.474 align:center line:79%
And so it should be for each
of us as instruments in His hands,

11:51.474 --> 11:54.444 align:center line:79%
in His amazing work
of salvation and exaltation.

11:54.711 --> 11:59.482 align:center line:79%
Miracles in the lives of real people
will result. In the name of Jesus Christ,

11:59.482 --> 12:00.483 align:center line:81%
amen.

